  "Area": "Unclassified",
  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "Suppose that \\(a\\) cows give \\(b\\) gallons of milk in \\(c\\) days. At this rate, how many gallons of milk will \\(d\\) cows give in \\(e\\) days?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{bde}{ac}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{ac}{bde}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{abde}{c}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{bcde}{a}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{abc}{de}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "At the stated constant rate, milk production scales with both the number of cows and the number of days. The original numbers of cows and days satisfy \\(a>0\\) and \\(c>0\\), so dividing \\(b\\) by \\(a\\) and then by \\(c\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{ac}\n\\]\ngallons per cow per day. Multiplying this rate by \\(d\\) cows and \\(e\\) days gives\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{ac}\\cdot d\\cdot e\n=\\boxed{\\frac{bde}{ac}}\n\\]\ngallons, choice \\(\\mathrm{A}\\).",
